{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high vs. realized prices (often by ~1–4 €/t), especially from ~day 7 onward; apply a downward level shift to the whole path, larger for mid-horizons.",
    "Excessive mean-reversion strength: model pulls prices back up too quickly after declines, while realized series stays lower for longer; dampen upward reversion and extend the duration of downtrends.",
    "Underestimation of drawdown depth: in selloffs the model does not go low enough (realized troughs are deeper by ~2–4 €/t); increase downside amplitude when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Horizon-shape error: early horizon (days 1–3) is relatively close, but mid-horizon (days ~5–20) is systematically too elevated; use a horizon-dependent bias correction (small near-term, larger mid-term).",
    "Late-horizon rebound miss: when a sharp rebound occurs late in the window (as in Example 5), the model can remain anchored too low; add a conditional rebound adjustment when recent realized volatility/momentum turns strongly positive near the end of history."
  ]
}
